# Ecological & Spatial Stewards in Grassroots Communities
#
# Community garden managers, library caretakers, urban foresters, tool library facilitators, and commons defenders.

#>spw_cache_comm_spatial_stewards
#:cache #!community_roles
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Model the physical space and ecological stewardship roles that heal degraded urban land, defend public commons, and restore reciprocal human-earth relations."
  ~#domains: "Urban agriculture, public libraries as civic sanctuaries, urban forestry, repair cafes, and anti-hostile architecture advocacy"
}

^["community_gardeners_and_soil_healers"]{
  mandate: "Transforming toxic brownfields and abandoned asphalt lots into thriving food forests and communal sanctuaries."
  core_practices: #[
    "Soil Remediation & Bioremediation: Using mycorrhizal fungi, sunflower hyperaccumulation, and heavy organic compost to bind heavy metals",
    "Heirloom Seed Saving & Gene Banking: Preserving rare ancestral maize, beans, squash, and medicinal herbs suited to microclimates",
    "Pollinator Corridors: Planting native wildflowers, milkweed, and flowering shrubs to create urban highways for bees and monarchs",
    "Communal Food Sovereignty: Distributing fresh organic greens, collards, tomatoes, and herbs freely to neighborhood families"
  ]
}

^["public_library_caretakers_and_commons_archivists"]{
  institution: "The public library as the supreme democratic institution—the only remaining indoor civic space where money is not required to exist."
  stewardship_roles: .{
    digital_equity_anchor: "Assisting elders, unhoused neighbors, and immigrants with public computers, printing, and job applications"
    warming_and_cooling_refuge: "Serving as life-saving climate sanctuaries during lethal summer heat domes and winter blizzards"
    seed_and_specialty_libraries: "Curating seed catalogs, musical instrument lending, sewing machine collections, and museum passes"
    literacy_and_esl_circles: "Facilitating volunteer adult reading tutoring and English conversation groups in welcoming community rooms"
  }[reg=facet]
}

^["urban_foresters_and_park_stewards"]{
  environmental_justice_focus: "Mitigating the deadly urban heat island effect in historically redlined neighborhoods that lack mature tree canopy."
  interventions: #[
    "Canopy Expansion Drives: Planting and watering drought-tolerant shade trees (oaks, maples, sycamores) along residential sidewalks",
    "Bioswales & Rain Gardens: Constructing curb cuts and native vegetation swales to capture storm runoff and prevent sewer overflows",
    "Adopt-a-Park Cleanups: Organizing regular weekend sweeps removing glass, plastic, and invasive species from neglected public parks"
  ]
}

^["tool_librarians_and_repair_café_hosts"]{
  anti_consumerist_ethos: "The de-commodification of physical equipment through collective ownership and shared mechanical mastery."
  activities: #[
    "Tool Lending Operations: Loaning power drills, tile saws, lawnmowers, and ladders free of charge to any resident with a library card",
    "Repair Cafés: Monthly volunteer sessions fixing broken toasters, lamps, bicycles, and winter jackets to divert tons of waste from landfills",
    "DIY Trade Workshops: Teaching neighbors basic residential plumbing, drywall patching, electrical wiring, and bicycle tuning"
  ]
}

^["commons_defenders_and_anti_hostile_architecture_activists"]{
  mandate: "Protecting the public sphere against spatial segregation, privatization, and hostile architectural deterrence."
  defensive_measures: #[
    "Bench Liberation: Installing and maintaining comfortable, undivided park benches where weary pedestrians and unhoused people can rest",
    "Public Hydration & Sanitation: Campaigning for clean, unlocked municipal restrooms and winterized public drinking water fountains",
    "Parklet & Plaza Reclamations: Converting redundant vehicular parking spaces into pedestrian seating parks filled with planters"
  ]
}

^["taste"]{
  ~#taste: "The health of human souls is intimately bound to the living soil and shared physical commons they walk upon."
  ~#wonder: "Why does the presence of a flourishing community garden on a previously vacant lot correlate with a dramatic drop in neighborhood gun violence?"

  principles: #[
    "Earth care is community care: Tending to a vegetable bed or a street tree is an act of civic love that heals both the steward and the block.",
    "The commons are sacred: Public land belongs to the collective human family and must never be surrendered to private enclosure or hostile design.",
    "Repair over replacement: Treating tools, appliances, and relationships with the patience and reverence required to mend them."
  ]
}

^"invariants"{
  "Community garden harvest distribution must prioritize food sovereignty and access for low-income neighbors.",
  "Public library and commons services must never require financial payment or commercial affiliation to enter and utilize.",
  "Urban forestry efforts must prioritize tree planting in areas with documented canopy deficits and elevated summer temperatures.",
}

#>wonder_comm_spatial_stewards_1
?["How does the conversion of vacant urban parcels into community gardens alter neighborhood violent crime and mental health indices?"]{
  #:depth #!sociological ~#lens(spatial_epidemiology)
  ~#hypothesis: "Greening and active stewardship of vacant urban lots leads to a 29% reduction in gun violence incidents within a half-mile radius over a 36-month period."
  !probe{ "Conduct difference-in-differences econometric analysis of police incident reports around newly established community gardens vs matched control lots." }
  $%[safety.gun_violence_delta, health.resident_perceived_safety_index]
}
